# The catalog is a signed artifact. Its minisign signature covers the exact
# bytes of the file, so any line-ending conversion invalidates it.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf` is on by default on Windows, including on GitHub's
# windows runners, so a fresh clone there rewrote catalog.json with CRLF and
# produced a catalog the hub refuses to load. The symptom was fourteen
# signature failures that named everything except the cause, and it was
# invisible locally: a file a tool writes and commits is never re-checked-out,
# so it keeps the endings it was written with.
#
# `-text` disables conversion in both directions while leaving the file
# diffable, which `binary` would not.
catalog/catalog.json           -text
catalog/catalog.json.minisig   -text
catalog/catalog-signing.pub    -text

# The Workflow tool's permission handler rejects a script containing CR bytes
# outright -- "script contains control characters that would be hidden in the
# approval dialog" -- so a CRLF workflow script cannot be launched at all, not
# merely displayed oddly. That is unlike the rest of this repository, which is
# CRLF by convention; these two globs are a deliberate exception, forced to LF
# in both directions so a fresh checkout on this Windows machine (where
# `core.autocrlf` defaults on) cannot flip them back and reintroduce the bug.
# `eol=lf` normalizes on checkout as well as on commit, unlike the `-text`
# entries above which merely stop conversion; two globs because gitattributes
# patterns don't support brace alternation (`*.{js,mjs}` is a shell syntax,
# not a gitignore/gitattributes one).
.claude/workflows/*.js    text eol=lf
.claude/workflows/*.mjs   text eol=lf
